select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p1-071","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"Why is life-cycle costing better than comparing purchase prices?","back":"It includes the operating (energy), maintenance and disposal costs over the asset's whole life, not just the first cost.","explain":"For motors, pumps and compressors, energy cost over the life can be many times the purchase price. A cheaper but inefficient unit can therefore cost far more in total.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q20","order":1323},
{"id":"p1-072","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"problem","front":"A 37 kW pump motor runs at 70% load with 90% efficiency for 20 h/day, 330 days/year. A VFD costing ₹2,50,000 is expected to cut its energy use by 15%. Power costs ₹8/kWh. Find the annual energy use, savings, simple payback and ROI.","back":"Energy ≈ 1,89,933 kWh/yr; savings ≈ 28,490 kWh = ₹2,27,920/yr; payback ≈ 1.1 years (~13 months); ROI ≈ 91%.","explain":"Input power = rated output × load ÷ efficiency. Savings are a percentage of current consumption. Payback and ROI are reciprocals; a payback near one year makes this an attractive low-risk measure.","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the motor input power.","answer":"37 × 0.70 ÷ 0.90 = 28.78 kW","explain":"Input = output × loading ÷ efficiency."},{"prompt":"Find the annual energy use.","answer":"28.78 × 20 × 330 = 1,89,933 kWh/yr","explain":"Energy = power × operating hours (6,600 h)."},{"prompt":"Find the energy and cost savings.","answer":"1,89,933 × 0.15 = 28,490 kWh; × ₹8 = ₹2,27,920/yr","explain":"Savings = % reduction × energy × tariff."},{"prompt":"Find the simple payback.","answer":"2,50,000 ÷ 2,27,920 = 1.10 years","explain":"Payback = investment ÷ annual savings."},{"prompt":"Find the ROI.","answer":"2,27,920 ÷ 2,50,000 × 100 = 91.2%","explain":"ROI = annual savings ÷ investment × 100."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 S6 (modified)","order":1324},
{"id":"p1-073","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"problem","front":"Two mutually exclusive projects each have a 5-year life. Project A costs ₹5 lakh and saves ₹1.6 lakh/yr. Project B costs ₹9 lakh and saves ₹2.75 lakh/yr. At a 12% discount rate, compare them by simple payback and by NPV. Which should be chosen if funds are not limited?","back":"Payback: A 3.13 yr, B 3.27 yr. NPV: A ≈ ₹0.77 lakh, B ≈ ₹0.91 lakh. Choose B (higher NPV).","explain":"Simple payback favours A, but payback ignores the time value of money and the absolute value created. NPV shows that B adds more wealth. With no capital constraint, NPV decides.","steps":[{"prompt":"Compute the simple paybacks.","answer":"A: 5 ÷ 1.6 = 3.13 yr; B: 9 ÷ 2.75 = 3.27 yr","explain":"Payback = cost ÷ annual savings."},{"prompt":"Find the cumulative PV factor at 12% for 5 years.","answer":"1/1.12 + 1/1.12² + … + 1/1.12⁵ = 0.893+0.797+0.712+0.636+0.567 = 3.605","explain":"Uniform annual savings: PV = savings × Σ 1/(1+i)ᵗ."},{"prompt":"Compute NPV of A.","answer":"1.6 × 3.605 − 5 = 5.768 − 5 = ₹0.77 lakh","explain":"NPV = PV of inflows − investment."},{"prompt":"Compute NPV of B.","answer":"2.75 × 3.605 − 9 = 9.913 − 9 = ₹0.91 lakh","explain":""},{"prompt":"Decide.","answer":"NPV(B) > NPV(A), so choose B, even though its payback is longer.","explain":"With no capital limit, select the project with the higher NPV."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 L5 (modified)","order":1325},
{"id":"p1-074","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"problem","front":"An energy-efficiency project costs ₹10 lakh and gives net savings of ₹3 lakh per year for 5 years, with no salvage value. Find the IRR by interpolation between 15% and 16%.","back":"IRR ≈ 15.24%.","explain":"IRR is the rate at which NPV = 0. Calculate NPV at two rates either side of zero and interpolate linearly. If the company's cost of capital is below 15.24%, the project is acceptable.","steps":[{"prompt":"NPV at 15%.","answer":"PV factor sum (5 yr, 15%) = 3.3522; NPV = 3 × 3.3522 − 10 = +₹0.0565 lakh","explain":"NPV = Σ CF/(1+r)ᵗ − investment."},{"prompt":"NPV at 16%.","answer":"PV factor sum (5 yr, 16%) = 3.2743; NPV = 3 × 3.2743 − 10 = −₹0.1771 lakh","explain":"The sign has changed, so the IRR lies between 15% and 16%."},{"prompt":"Interpolate.","answer":"IRR = 15 + 0.0565 × (16 − 15) ÷ (0.0565 + 0.1771) = 15 + 0.24 = 15.24%","explain":"IRR = r₁ + NPV₁ (r₂ − r₁) ÷ (NPV₁ − NPV₂)."},{"prompt":"Decide.","answer":"Accept if the cost of capital is below 15.24%.","explain":"IRR > cost of capital → project adds value."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 L2, S4 (modified)","order":1326},
{"id":"p1-b07-001","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"According to the BEE guide book, what is the biggest constraint in increasing the number of energy projects?","back":"Lack of finance.","explain":"The book notes that alternative finance arrangements can overcome the 'initial cost' obstacle and let firms implement more energy management proposals. It also observes that many energy managers rely only on simple payback, which does not reveal the added value of after-tax benefits.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p193","order":1327},
{"id":"p1-b07-002","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"When an energy auditor puts forward a proposal, what two questions will management ask, and why do they matter?","back":"How much will the proposal cost? How much money will it save? The energy project competes with many other projects for limited capital.","explain":"Senior management's job is to put capital where it earns the greatest return, so an energy project is only one of many candidates. The book notes that in practice management often demands a higher rate of return from energy projects than from core, directly profit-making investments.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p193","order":1328},
{"id":"p1-b07-003","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"True or false: an organisation should demand a faster rate of return from energy-efficiency investments than from its other investments.","back":"False. Energy-efficiency investments should be judged by exactly the same criteria as all other investments.","explain":"Section 7.3 says investment in energy efficiency is in most respects no different from any other area of financial management, so a faster or more attractive return should not be demanded. (Section 7.2 observes that management often does demand a higher return in practice; the book's recommendation is that it should not.)","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p194","order":1329},
{"id":"p1-b07-004","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"Before making any energy-saving investment, what should be ensured first?","back":"That existing plant gives its best performance, energy is bought at the lowest possible tariffs, the best energy forms (fuel or electricity) are used efficiently, and good housekeeping is practised regularly.","explain":"Low-cost technical and housekeeping measures often cut waste without capital. Spending money before these are in place can mean paying for savings that were available for free.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p194","order":1330},
{"id":"p1-b07-005","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"Besides energy savings, what benefits of an energy project should be presented to senior management?","back":"Lower operating costs, low risk/reward ratio, reduced environmental cost, improved productivity, better product or service quality, and potential to improve the company's share value.","explain":"Framing the project in these business terms makes it easier to compete for capital with other proposals. The book adds that a systematic financial approach must still be used to rate options against expected savings.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p194","order":1331},
{"id":"p1-b07-006","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"What are the three groups of basic criteria for financial investment appraisal, and what does each tell you?","back":"1) Payback period – how long until the investment recovers itself (sets the needed financing term). 2) NPV and cash flow – financial planning of money inflows and outflows over time. 3) ROI and IRR – comparison with other investment options.","explain":"Each criterion answers a different management question. Payback is about liquidity and loan term; NPV and cash flow fit the project into the corporate financial system; ROI and IRR express returns as a rate that can be compared with alternatives and the cost of money.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p194","order":1332},
{"id":"p1-b07-007","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"Why is the word 'simple' used in 'simple payback period'?","back":"Because the time value of money is not considered in its calculation.","explain":"Simple payback = capital cost ÷ annual net savings, where annual net savings are the savings left after all operating costs. Cash inflows are just added without discounting.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p195","order":1333},
{"id":"p1-b07-008","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"What are the two limitations of ROI given in the BEE book?","back":"It ignores the time value of money, and it does not account for year-to-year variation in net cash inflows.","explain":"In the book's example (₹1,00,000 outlay, ₹25,000 a year) the 25% ROI would be strictly valid only if ₹25,000 came in every year in perpetuity – not a realistic condition.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p196","order":1334},
{"id":"p1-b07-009","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"If money earns 10% interest, what is ₹100 today worth in one year, and what is ₹100 received one year from now worth today?","back":"₹110 in one year; ₹90.91 today.","explain":"FV = PV × (1 + i)ⁿ and PV = FV ÷ (1 + i)ⁿ. So 100 × 1.1 = 110, and 100 ÷ 1.1 = 90.91. Relating cash flows at different times in this way is called discounting, or the present value concept.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p197","order":1335},
{"id":"p1-b07-010","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"State the NPV decision rule. What does a zero NPV mean?","back":"Accept if NPV is positive, reject if negative. A zero NPV is value-neutral.","explain":"A negative NPV means the project does not reach the required return and would cause an economic loss if implemented. Among alternatives, the higher the NPV the more attractive the project.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p198","order":1336},
{"id":"p1-b07-011","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"What should the discount rate used in NPV reflect, and how should it be set relative to the borrowing rate?","back":"It should reflect the risk of the project; it is prudent to set it slightly above the interest rate at which the capital is borrowed.","explain":"The credibility of NPV depends entirely on a realistic discount rate, which is often hard to predict. Setting it a little above the cost of borrowing builds in a margin.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p198","order":1337},
{"id":"p1-b07-012","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"When is an investment judged sound by IRR, and how is IRR used to choose among alternatives?","back":"It is sound if the IRR is greater than the current interest rate. Among alternatives, choose the one with the highest rate of return.","explain":"IRR is the discount rate that makes NPV = 0. Finding it is iterative (trial and error) and usually done with a spreadsheet; Excel has an IRR function.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p200","order":1338},
{"id":"p1-b07-013","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"Give the interpolation formula for IRR.","back":"IRR = lower rate + [NPV at lower rate × (higher rate − lower rate)] ÷ (NPV at lower rate − NPV at higher rate).","explain":"Find one discount rate giving a positive NPV and a nearby rate giving a negative NPV, then interpolate linearly between them. The result can also be read from a graph of NPV against discount rate where the curve crosses zero.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p201","order":1339},
{"id":"p1-b07-014","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"What limitation of IRR does the BEE book state?","back":"IRR cannot distinguish between lending and borrowing, so a high IRR is not necessarily a desirable feature.","explain":"Its advantages are that it accounts for the time value of money, uses the whole cash-flow stream, and gives a rate of return that business people find easier to think about than an absolute NPV figure.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p202","order":1340},
{"id":"p1-b07-015","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"What is the key difference between the NPV and IRR methods?","back":"NPV assumes the discount rate (cost of capital) is known and finds the value; IRR sets NPV to zero and finds the discount rate. NPV is a tool for comparing several projects; IRR tests whether a single project reaches a target rate of return.","explain":"Both discount cash flows, so they look similar. But they answer different questions: 'how much value at this rate?' versus 'what rate does this project earn?'","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p202","order":1341},
{"id":"p1-b07-016","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"How do expenses differ from capital investments, and what do initial capital costs include?","back":"Expenses are routine, ongoing costs of normal operation; capital investments are strategic, long-term, usually decided by senior management and taxed differently. Initial capital cost includes purchase plus installation and preparation costs.","explain":"Initial costs are usually non-recurring and may be spent at once or over several years. Funds for capital projects are generally limited, which is why appraisal is needed.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p203","order":1342},
{"id":"p1-b07-017","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"What are the conventions and requirements of a cash flow diagram?","back":"Time runs along the horizontal axis; upward arrows are inflows (revenue or savings), downward arrows are outflows (costs); arrows point away from the axis and can be summed within a year. A good diagram is complete, accurate and legible.","explain":"The economic life sets the horizontal scale, usually in years. Showing timing clearly makes it more likely that time-value-of-money concepts are applied correctly.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p204","order":1343},
{"id":"p1-b07-018","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"A project is feasible with energy cost savings escalating at 10% a year, but sensitivity analysis shows break-even at 9%. What does this mean?","back":"The project carries a high degree of risk, because it becomes unviable if energy-cost inflation falls only slightly, below 9%.","explain":"Sensitivity analysis is recommended especially for marginal projects. It identifies uncertain parameters to which the NPV or IRR decision is sensitive, and 'switching values' show how much a variable must change to flip acceptance to rejection.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p205","order":1344},
{"id":"p1-b07-019","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"In sensitivity analysis, what is the difference between micro and macro factors? Give examples.","back":"Micro factors are within the firm's control: operating expenses, capital structure, cost of debt and equity, form of finance (e.g. leasing), project life. Macro factors are economy-wide and cannot be changed by management: interest rates, tax rates, accounting standards, depreciation rates, regulations, energy prices, technology changes.","explain":"Macro variables affect the whole industry in which the company operates. The book also lists extension of government-subsidised projects and general employment or salary trends as macro factors.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p205","order":1345},
{"id":"p1-b07-020","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"Why is the cost of equity (stock) higher than the cost of debt financing?","back":"Equity holders share in profits, and interest on debt is tax-deductible while stock dividends are not.","explain":"The two main sources of debt capital are loans and bonds; the two main sources of equity are stocks and retained earnings. Retained earnings carry the same cost of capital as stock because they truly belong to the stockholders.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p206","order":1346},
{"id":"p1-b07-021","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"How does a capital lease differ from a true lease?","back":"A capital lease is midway between debt and equity and gives a lower cost of capital with third-party participation. A true lease gives use of equipment without ownership risk, suits short-term use, has tax-deductible payments, but gives no depreciation benefit and no ownership even at the end.","explain":"A true lease reduces the risk of poor performance, poor service and obsolescence because the lessee never owns the asset.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p207","order":1347},
{"id":"p1-b07-022","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"What is the unique feature of energy performance contracting, and why does it lead to a wider scope of work?","back":"The contractor is paid only from the performance of the installed equipment, i.e. after it actually reduces expenses. This removes any incentive to cut corners and encourages exceeding savings, so contracts tend to be facility-wide.","explain":"With a facility-wide scope, several improvements (e.g. lighting and air conditioning) can be done together, giving a 'multiplier effect' on cost reduction. The client invests very little money up-front.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p207","order":1348},
{"id":"p1-b07-023","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"In a fixed-fee ESCO contract, what does the ESCO do and how does its risk compare with savings-based contracts?","back":"It audits, designs and either helps implement or simply advises, for a fixed lump-sum fee. It bears less risk because its fee does not depend on the savings achieved.","explain":"The three common ESCO options are fixed fee, shared savings and guaranteed savings; a part-fixed-fee, part-shared-savings combination is also used. Guaranteed savings gives the client most security but usually costs more.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p208","order":1349},
{"id":"p1-b07-024","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"Which of these is NOT a typical performance contract: shared savings, guaranteed savings, fixed fee, hire purchase?","back":"Hire purchase.","explain":"Shared savings, guaranteed savings and fixed fee are the three ESCO contract options in the BEE book. Hire purchase is an ordinary purchase-finance arrangement whose payments do not depend on energy savings.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p208","order":1350},
{"id":"p1-b07-025","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"What are the pros and cons of performance contracting for the host company?","back":"Pros: use of equipment with reduced installation/operating risk, poor performance, service and obsolescence risk; host can focus on its core business. Cons: potentially binding contracts, legal expenses, higher administrative cost, and savings must be shared.","explain":"Tax benefits of depreciation must also be negotiated. Because complex contracts leave more room for error, the book stresses choosing an ESCO with a good reputation and experience in similar facilities.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p208","order":1351},
{"id":"p1-b07-026","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"What three conditions must an asset meet to be depreciable?","back":"It must be held by the business to produce income, it must wear out or be consumed in use, and it must have a life longer than one year.","explain":"Depreciation allowances are deductions from taxable income that tax law permits because most business assets lose value over time.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p209","order":1352},
{"id":"p1-b07-027","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"List the services an ESCO usually offers.","back":"Investment grade energy audit; financing (own funds or via banks); purchase, installation and maintenance of the equipment; training of O&M staff; monitoring of operations and savings; measurement and savings verification; and a guarantee of savings.","explain":"The investment grade audit identifies savings, assesses risks and mitigation, and calculates cost-effectiveness over time. ESCO services have become customer-driven, so the client picks from this menu.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p209","order":1353},
{"id":"p1-b07-028","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"What benefits does an industry get from working with an ESCO?","back":"Immediate upgrade and lower operating costs without initial capital; access to ESCO expertise; positive cash flow; capital freed for core business; better O&M; ESCO takes on several business risks including guaranteed equipment performance for the contract life; services paid from money otherwise spent on wasted energy.","explain":"The guarantee covers performance for the whole contract, not just a warranty period. The book also mentions a more comfortable, productive environment.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p210","order":1354},
{"id":"p1-b07-029","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"What is an investment grade energy audit (IGA), and how do single-step and two-step ESCO engagements differ?","back":"An IGA identifies efficiency opportunities and translates the technical findings into financial terms, presenting a bankable project that can secure a loan. Single step: ESCO does the IGA and implements the measures. Two step: ESCO only does the IGA and reports; the organisation prioritises measures.","explain":"In the two-step route the organisation ranks measures by time, capital investment and payback period. If a bank finances the project, detailed cash flow, IRR, debt service coverage ratio and sensitivity analysis are prepared.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p210","order":1355},
{"id":"p1-b07-030","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"How does contract duration affect the risk of an ESCO contract?","back":"The longer the duration, the higher the risk and uncertainty.","explain":"Contracts have rewards and penalties built in through guarantees. The organisation can contract with the ESCO under one of three payment mechanisms: fixed fee, shared savings or guaranteed savings.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p210","order":1356},
{"id":"p1-b07-031","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"How are energy savings calculated in a performance contract, and how are energy price changes handled?","back":"Energy saved = Baseline − Current ± Adjustments. Cost savings use a price agreed in the contract (a set price, or a formula such as average monthly energy cost) so they reflect only the efficiency measures.","explain":"Adjustments bring current-period energy use to the same conditions as the baseline period. Without an agreed price, savings in rupees would swing with tariff changes that have nothing to do with the project.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p211","order":1357},
{"id":"p1-b07-032","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"In the BEE case study of the ABC building ESCO project, what were the baseline consumption, savings potential and guaranteed savings?","back":"Baseline about 21,28,545 kWh/year; potential 39% reduction in energy use and 33% in the energy bill; guarantee of at least 7,93,250 kWh/year, which is 95% of the proposed ≈8,35,000 kWh saving.","explain":"Main loads were lighting and air conditioning. Measures included task lighting, replacing window/split ACs with a centrifugal-chiller central AC (also used for winter heating), efficient pumps, and LPG instead of electric heating in the canteen.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p212","order":1358},
{"id":"p1-b07-033","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"In the BEE ABC building case study, what were the contract term and savings-sharing ratios?","back":"5-year term. Savings shared 95% contractor : 5% owner for the first three years, and 90% : 10% in years four and five; the assets then pass to the owner at a nominal cost.","explain":"The owner also pays a fixed sum for maintaining the measures. Contractor payments are tied to actual bill reduction, so the contractor also carries out O&M, and whole-facility performance measurement and verification is done jointly.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p213","order":1359},
{"id":"p1-b07-034","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"Why is performance contracting attractive for municipalities, and what is the estimated saving potential in municipal water pumping?","back":"Municipal budgets lack funds for efficiency upgrades; performance contracting needs no upfront investment because the cost is paid from savings. Water pumping alone has at least 25% saving potential.","explain":"ESCOs bring managerial, technical and turnkey skills plus the ability to structure financing. The steps include a walk-through self-assessment, an Expression of Interest (EOI), a Request for Proposal (RFP) to firms that responded, IGA, contract, and monitoring and verification.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p214","order":1360},
{"id":"p1-b07-035","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"problem","front":"A cogeneration system will cut a company's annual energy bill by ₹23 lakh. It costs ₹90 lakh, and annual operating and maintenance costs are ₹5 lakh. Find the simple payback period.","back":"5 years.","explain":"Annual net savings must deduct the operating costs from the gross bill reduction. Using ₹23 lakh instead of ₹18 lakh would wrongly give about 3.9 years.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p195","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the annual net savings.","answer":"23 − 5 = ₹18 lakh/year","explain":"Simple payback uses net savings: benefits minus yearly costs."},{"prompt":"Write the formula.","answer":"Simple payback = capital cost ÷ annual net savings","explain":"Time value of money is ignored, hence 'simple'."},{"prompt":"Calculate.","answer":"90 ÷ 18 = 5 years","explain":"After 5 years the capital is recovered and later savings are clear profit."}],"order":1361},
{"id":"p1-b07-036","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"problem","front":"Projects A and B each cost ₹1,00,000. A saves ₹50,000, 30,000, 20,000, 10,000, 10,000 in years 1–5. B saves ₹20,000, 20,000, 20,000, 40,000, 50,000, 60,000 in years 1–6. Find each payback and say what it shows about the payback method.","back":"A: 3 years; B: 4 years. Payback prefers A even though B has much larger inflows in years 5 and 6 – it ignores savings after payback.","explain":"This is the book's example of the main drawback of payback: it ignores everything after the payback point. B's total savings (₹2,10,000) far exceed A's (₹1,20,000).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p195","steps":[{"prompt":"Cumulate A's savings until they reach ₹1,00,000.","answer":"50,000 + 30,000 + 20,000 = 1,00,000 at end of year 3 → 3 years","explain":"Payback is the time to recover the investment."},{"prompt":"Cumulate B's savings.","answer":"20,000 × 3 = 60,000; + 40,000 = 1,00,000 at end of year 4 → 4 years","explain":"B recovers its cost one year later."},{"prompt":"Compare total savings.","answer":"A: ₹1,20,000; B: ₹2,10,000","explain":"B's large later inflows are ignored by payback."},{"prompt":"Conclude.","answer":"Payback picks A, though B is clearly better over its life","explain":"Payback favours early cash inflows and discriminates against projects with large later inflows."}],"order":1362},
{"id":"p1-b07-037","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"problem","front":"An outlay of ₹1,00,000 on equipment gives an after-tax cash flow of ₹25,000 a year for six years, with no significant fluctuation. Find the ROI.","back":"25%.","explain":"ROI = annual net cash flow ÷ capital cost × 100. The book warns this 25% is strictly valid only if ₹25,000 came every year in perpetuity, because ROI ignores the time value of money.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 07 p196","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the formula.","answer":"ROI = annual net cash flow ÷ capital cost × 100","explain":"ROI is the inverse of payback, as a percentage."},{"prompt":"Substitute.","answer":"25,000 ÷ 1,00,000 × 100","explain":"Use the average annual operating cash flow and net investment."},{"prompt":"Calculate and judge.","answer":"ROI = 25%; it must exceed the cost of money to be attractive","explain":"Payback here is 4 years, the inverse of 25%."}],"order":1363}
]$cards$::jsonb);
